        question: 'Operating Buildkite agent fleets — autoscaling, spot, monitoring.',
        answer: `Production agent fleet operations are where Buildkite teams spend real engineering time. The patterns:

Architecture. Buildkite Agent Scaler watches queue depth via Buildkite API; adjusts the underlying pool size (ASG, Karpenter NodePool, K8s replicas). Agents register with Buildkite on startup; deregister on shutdown.

Three deployment patterns:

Pattern 1: EC2 ASG with custom AMI. Pre-bake an AMI with Docker, agent binary, common toolchains. ASG launches instances; instance start script registers agent and starts polling. Buildkite Agent Scaler adjusts ASG desired-count based on queue depth.

Pros: simple, well-understood. Spot instances trivial via mixed-instance ASG.
Cons: agents are long-lived; persistent state; supply-chain footgun. Mitigations: shutdown-after-job: true makes them ephemeral but slow (full instance startup per job, 30-60s cold-start).

Pattern 2: Kubernetes with Helm chart. Buildkite-agent runs as a Deployment. Each pod polls for jobs. shutdown-after-job: true makes them per-job (pod terminates at end). Karpenter scales nodes.

Pros: ephemeral pods (no supply-chain footgun); fast cold-start (~5-10s); cluster-autoscaler / Karpenter handles capacity.
Cons: requires K8s expertise.

Pattern 3: Buildkite Hosted Agents (announced 2024). Buildkite-managed agent pools. Limited adoption; runs counter to Buildkite's "your infrastructure" pitch but useful for small projects that want zero ops.

Production EKS pattern with Karpenter:

\`\`\`yaml
# Karpenter NodePool for Buildkite agents
apiVersion: karpenter.sh/v1
kind: NodePool
metadata: { name: buildkite-build }
spec:
  template:
    metadata:
      labels: { workload: buildkite-build }
    spec:
      taints: [{ key: workload, value: buildkite-build, effect: NoSchedule }]
      nodeClassRef: { name: buildkite-nodes, group: karpenter.k8s.aws, kind: EC2NodeClass }
      requirements:
        - { key: node.kubernetes.io/instance-type, operator: In, values: [m5.xlarge, m5.2xlarge, m5.4xlarge, m5a.xlarge, m5n.xlarge] }
        - { key: karpenter.sh/capacity-type, operator: In, values: [spot, on-demand] }
        - { key: kubernetes.io/arch, operator: In, values: [amd64] }
  limits:
    cpu: 1000
  disruption:
    consolidationPolicy: WhenEmpty
    consolidateAfter: 1m
\`\`\`

Buildkite agents run as a Deployment with tolerations matching the taint. Mixed-instance + spot capacity = cost-optimized fleet that auto-consolidates.

Multiple NodePools per workload type:
- buildkite-build: m5/m5a/m5n family, spot-preferred.
- buildkite-arm: m6g/m6gd family.
- buildkite-gpu: g5 family, on-demand only (spot interruption hurts ML).
- buildkite-deploy: small instances, on-demand only (don't interrupt deploys).

Autoscaler config. Buildkite ships buildkite-agent-scaler that runs as a sidecar service:

\`\`\`yaml
- queue: build
  min: 5                      # minimum idle capacity (warm agents for fast pickup)
  max: 200
  scaleUpFactor: 1.5          # scale 1.5x faster than queue grows
  scaleDownFactor: 0.5        # scale down conservatively
\`\`\`

Min capacity matters for time-sensitive PRs — cold-start lag delays first feedback. 5-10 warm agents per queue typical.

Monitoring. Key metrics:
- Queue depth per queue (alerting: >20 for >5 minutes)
- Agent count per queue
- Job dispatch latency (queue time)
- Job success/failure rate per queue
- Agent shutdown reasons (graceful vs OOM vs spot-preempt)

Buildkite emits these via API + Prometheus exporter. Grafana dashboards via the buildkite-agent-metrics project.

Spot interruption handling. K8s pod with shutdown-after-job: true tolerates Karpenter node consolidation gracefully. Long jobs need:
- Checkpoint-and-resume (application-level).
- retryStrategy at pipeline level: { automatic: { exit_status: -1, limit: 3 } } — auto-retry on agent loss.
- Reserve on-demand for jobs that don't tolerate interruption (deploys, prod tests).

Cost optimization at scale:
- Karpenter spot consolidation.
- Right-size instances per queue (don't run all builds on m5.4xlarge if most need m5.large).
- Reserved Instances or Savings Plans for baseline capacity.
- Aggressive cache (Docker layer cache via type=registry, dependency cache via S3 backed by EFS for shared mount).

Real-world numbers: 200-engineer org running ~10k builds/day. Spot-heavy m5 fleet. ~$15k/month EKS compute. Equivalent on GitHub-hosted runners would be 5-10x more expensive.

Operational maturity required. This is real platform engineering — bookings of 1-2 engineers operating it. For shops without that bandwidth, GHA Pro is the better answer despite higher per-minute cost.`,
