        answer: `Rapid-fire facts.

Q: What's Buildkite's value proposition?
A: Hybrid SaaS + self-hosted agents. SaaS UX without giving up infrastructure control. Designed for mega-monorepo CI.

Q: Where do builds run?
A: On your agents, on your infrastructure (EC2, EKS, on-prem). Buildkite hosts only the orchestrator and UI.

Q: Killer differentiator?
A: Dynamic pipeline generation. Pipelines emitted by scripts at runtime in any language. Makes mega-monorepos manageable.

Q: Agent fleets?
A: Logical groupings of agents with tags. Multiple fleets per workload type (build, kaniko, deploy, gpu, arm). Jobs target via agents: queue/role/etc.

Q: Pricing model?
A: Per-build (job count × seats), not per-minute. Compute costs are yours (EC2/EKS); Buildkite charges for orchestration only.

Q: Major Buildkite users?
A: Shopify, Airbnb, Pinterest, Etsy, Block (Square), Canva, Uber, PagerDuty.

Q: Plugin ecosystem?
A: ~200 plugins (vs GHA's ~25k actions). Common: docker, docker-compose, aws-assume-role-with-web-identity, test-collector, artifacts, monorepo-diff. Custom plugins easy to author.

Q: OIDC support?
A: Yes, since 2023. aws-assume-role-with-web-identity plugin handles federation.

Q: Test Engine?
A: Paid Buildkite product for timing-based test splitting + flaky-test detection + analytics. Works with any CI platform via test-collector plugin.

Q: Block step?
A: Manual approval gate. Pipeline pauses until a designated user clicks Continue. Equivalent of GHA Environments.

Q: Wait step?
A: Synchronization barrier. All preceding steps must complete before any following start.

Q: Monorepo-diff plugin?
A: Skips steps based on git diff against base branch. Plugin-level path filtering.

Q: When does Buildkite win over GHA + ARC?
A: Mega-monorepos (100+ services); dynamic pipelines essential; multi-SCM; need full agent fleet specialization; team comfortable operating fleet infrastructure.

Q: When does GHA + ARC win over Buildkite?
A: On GitHub; mid-scale; want polished marketplace; cost-conscious at moderate volume.

Q: Annotations?
A: Markdown content posted by agents during builds. Appears inline in build UI. Used for build summaries, test results, perf graphs.

Q: Custom plugin authoring?
A: Any git repo with plugin.yml manifest + Bash hook scripts (pre-checkout, environment, pre-command, post-command, etc.). Versioned by git tag.

Q: Spot instance support?
A: Native via underlying AWS/GCP autoscalers. Ephemeral agents tolerate spot preemption with proper retry config.

Q: Operational responsibility?
A: Yours. Agent fleets, autoscaling, monitoring, security updates — all your platform team's job. Real ops investment.

Q: Most common Buildkite mistake?
A: Static pipeline.yml at scale; persistent (non-ephemeral) agents; single-fleet for all workload types.

These are the answers a Buildkite-fluent platform engineer should give without preparation.`,
